1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,406
All five Chai Corner outlets
need fresh milk every morning.

2
00:00:04,706 --> 00:00:06,580
Three dairies want the deal.

3
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:08,261
Ravi must pick just one.

4
00:00:08,811 --> 00:00:10,624
Kabir says, take the cheapest.

5
00:00:10,924 --> 00:00:12,959
Asha says, take the best milk.

6
00:00:12,959 --> 00:00:14,892
Meena says, take the closest.

7
00:00:15,192 --> 00:00:18,005
Everyone has a reason, and nobody agrees.

8
00:00:18,655 --> 00:00:19,776
No guessing.

9
00:00:19,776 --> 00:00:21,256
No loudest voice.

10
00:00:21,256 --> 00:00:22,677
Just pure logic.

11
00:00:23,377 --> 00:00:25,768
So, what is rational decision making?

12
00:00:26,068 --> 00:00:30,731
Rational decision making means using
pure logic to choose the option that

13
00:00:30,731 --> 00:00:32,356
gives the most value.

14
00:00:32,956 --> 00:00:35,371
It pictures a perfect decision maker.

15
00:00:35,371 --> 00:00:36,421
Fully logical.

16
00:00:36,421 --> 00:00:37,834
With all the facts.

17
00:00:37,834 --> 00:00:41,113
Who knows every option,
and where each one leads.

18
00:00:41,463 --> 00:00:44,997
And it always picks the very
best, not just a good one.

19
00:00:44,997 --> 00:00:46,274
That is optimising.

20
00:00:46,924 --> 00:00:49,320
The rational model follows six steps.

21
00:00:49,770 --> 00:00:50,670
One.

22
00:00:50,312 --> 00:00:51,779
Define the problem.

23
00:00:51,779 --> 00:00:55,437
Chai Corner needs one milk
supplier for five outlets.

24
00:00:55,787 --> 00:00:56,687
Two.

25
00:00:56,338 --> 00:01:01,625
Identify the decision criteria: price,
quality, reliability, and distance.

26
00:01:01,975 --> 00:01:02,875
Three.

27
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:03,569
Weight them.

28
00:01:03,569 --> 00:01:06,263
Quality matters most, so it gets four.

29
00:01:06,263 --> 00:01:09,249
Reliability three,
price two, distance one.

30
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:10,499
Four.

31
00:01:10,191 --> 00:01:11,746
Generate alternatives.

32
00:01:11,746 --> 00:01:14,676
Green Farm, City Dairy, and Hill Farm.

33
00:01:15,026 --> 00:01:15,926
Five.

34
00:01:15,622 --> 00:01:19,671
Rate each alternative on each
criterion, from one to ten.

35
00:01:20,021 --> 00:01:21,635
Green Farm is cheapest.

36
00:01:21,635 --> 00:01:23,612
Hill Farm has the best milk.

37
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:26,347
City Dairy is never late, and close by.

38
00:01:26,697 --> 00:01:27,597
Six.

39
00:01:27,212 --> 00:01:28,607
Compute and choose.

40
00:01:28,607 --> 00:01:32,245
Multiply each rating by its
weight, then add them up.

41
00:01:32,595 --> 00:01:34,403
Green Farm scores sixty.

42
00:01:34,403 --> 00:01:36,264
Hill Farm, sixty seven.

43
00:01:36,264 --> 00:01:38,457
And City Dairy, seventy six.

44
00:01:38,807 --> 00:01:39,985
City Dairy wins.

45
00:01:39,985 --> 00:01:44,171
Not the cheapest, not the best
milk, but the most value overall.

46
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:47,825
But the rational model
makes four big assumptions.

47
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:49,075
One.

48
00:01:48,713 --> 00:01:50,340
The problem is clear.

49
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:51,540
Two.

50
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:52,902
Every option is known.

51
00:01:53,202 --> 00:01:54,102
Three.

52
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,476
Preferences are clear,
and they don't change.

53
00:01:56,776 --> 00:01:57,676
And four.

54
00:01:57,525 --> 00:01:59,881
There are no limits on time or money.

55
00:02:00,231 --> 00:02:02,523
In real life, these are rarely true.

56
00:02:02,873 --> 00:02:05,318
What about a fourth dairy nobody knew?

57
00:02:05,318 --> 00:02:07,182
Or a price jump next month?

58
00:02:07,832 --> 00:02:08,756
Quick check.

59
00:02:08,756 --> 00:02:12,496
Ravi has listed price, quality,
reliability, and distance.

60
00:02:12,496 --> 00:02:14,066
What is the next step?

61
00:02:15,966 --> 00:02:17,313
Weight the criteria!

62
00:02:17,313 --> 00:02:21,135
Decide how much each one matters,
before you rate any option.

63
00:02:21,735 --> 00:02:22,744
So, remember.

64
00:02:23,044 --> 00:02:27,902
Rational decision making uses pure
logic to pick the option with the most

65
00:02:27,902 --> 00:02:28,802
value.

66
00:02:28,913 --> 00:02:33,465
Six steps: define, set criteria,
weight them, list options, rate them,

67
00:02:33,465 --> 00:02:34,383
and compute.

68
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:38,891
But it assumes full information and
no limits, and that is rarely true.

69
00:02:39,491 --> 00:02:44,357
Next time, we'll see how real managers
decide when they cannot know everything.
